        <ArticleTitle>Locally injected autologous platelet-rich plasma enhanced tissue perfusion and improved survival of long subdermal plexus skin flaps in dogs.</ArticleTitle>
        <Pagination>
          <MedlinePgn>379-86</MedlinePgn>
        </Pagination>
        <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.3415/VCOT-14-02-0030</ELocationID>
        <Abstract>
          <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVES" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">Distal flap necrosis remains a major complication in subdermal plexus (random) skin flaps. Platelet-rich plasma (PRP) has been shown to improve the survival of ischemic random skin flaps in rats. The objective of this study was to evaluate the effect of locally injected autologous PRP on the survival of long (5:1 length-to-width ratio) subdermal plexus skin flaps in dogs.</AbstractText>
          <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">A 2x10 cm subdermal plexus skin flap was created bilaterally on the abdominal wall of six Beagle dogs. One randomly selected side received 2.5 ml of fresh auto-logous PRP injected evenly between sutures underneath the flap, whereas the other side was left untreated (control). Skin flap survival was evaluated macroscopically, histologically and by laser-Doppler flowmetry measurements of tissue perfusion.</AbstractText>
          <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Flap percentage survival on day 10 (96.3% versus 74.5%; p = 0.046) and tissue perfusion (p &lt;0.036) were significantly higher in PRP-treated flaps compared with controls. Histologically, there was less oedema in PRP-treated flaps compared to controls (p = 0.01), whereas collagen production and angiogenesis did not differ significantly between the two groups.</AbstractText>
          <AbstractText Label="CLINICAL SIGNIFICANCE" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The use of locally injected autologous PRP increases tissue perfusion and improves the survival of long subdermal plexus skin flaps in dogs.</AbstractText>
        </Abstract>
